---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "activation", label: "Activation" },
  { id: "sizes", label: "Sizes" },
  { id: "semantics", label: "Semantics" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="split_button">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A split button puts the action you take most of the time next to a
          menu of the ones you might take instead, such as Save and "save as",
          Reply and "reply all", or Export and every other format.
        </p>
        <p>
          The root is a <code>&lt;div class="split-button"&gt;</code> holding
          two <a href={route('buttons')}>buttons</a>: the leading half
          does the work, and the trailing half is a
          <a href={route('menu')}><code>menu-trigger</code></a> that
          opens a <code>&lt;menu&gt;</code>. The halves sit 2dp apart. The
          outer corners are round, and the inner corners at the seam stay
          nearly square until the user hovers, presses or opens the menu.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="split-button">
            <button class="button">Save</button>
            <button class="button menu-trigger" data-target="split-demo" aria-label="More save options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-demo">
              <li><button type="button">Save a copy</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Save as template</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Export as PDF</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="split-button">
  <button class="button">Save</button>
  <button class="button menu-trigger" data-target="save-menu" aria-label="More save options">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
  </button>
  <menu id="save-menu">
    <li><button type="button">Save a copy</button></li>
    <li><button type="button">Save as template</button></li>
    <li><button type="button">Export as PDF</button></li>
  </menu>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          The trailing half is an ordinary
          <a href={route('menu')}>Menu</a> trigger and behaves like any
          other: <code>Expressive.AutoInit()</code> starts it,
          <code>data-target</code> names the surface, and the menu keeps its
          own keyboard model, alignment and options. This component
          adds no script of its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here the menu sizes itself to its content. Menu normally matches the
          surface width to its trigger, but the surface cannot go below its
          112dp minimum, and this trigger is a chevron in a 48dp box. Items
          still wrap at the menu's 280dp maximum.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both halves are buttons, so every style class works on them. Use the
          same class on both, or the pair no longer looks like one shape. A
          <code>tonal</code> split button is the usual choice when the leading
          action is not the page's primary one.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="split-button">
            <button class="button tonal">Reply</button>
            <button class="button tonal menu-trigger" data-target="split-tonal" aria-label="More reply options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-tonal">
              <li><button type="button">Reply all</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Forward</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
          <div class="split-button">
            <button class="button outlined">Reply</button>
            <button class="button outlined menu-trigger" data-target="split-outlined" aria-label="More reply options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-outlined">
              <li><button type="button">Reply all</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Forward</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
        </div>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.activation}>
        <p>
          Opening the menu changes the trailing half in two ways. The chevron
          flips, and its inner corner becomes fully round, so the pair looks
          like two separate buttons while the menu is open. Both changes come
          from <code>aria-expanded</code>, which Menu sets, so you have nothing
          to toggle or author. Open the demo above and watch the corner.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hovering or pressing either half rounds that half's inner corner part
          of the way, using Material's values: 8dp at <code>xsmall</code>,
          12dp through <code>medium</code>, and 20dp at the two largest sizes.
          These changes do not animate under
          <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.sizes}>
        <p>
          The container takes one of five sizes: <code>xsmall</code>,
          <code>small</code> (the default), <code>medium</code>,
          <code>large</code> and <code>xlarge</code>. They are 32, 40, 56, 96
          and 136dp tall, the same heights as a <a href={route('buttons')}>common
          button</a>. Put the class on the <code>split-button</code>, never on
          a half. The container sizes both halves, and the seam, the insets
          and the chevron scale with it.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="split-button xsmall">
            <button class="button tonal">Extra small</button>
            <button class="button tonal menu-trigger" data-target="split-xsmall" aria-label="More options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-xsmall">
              <li><button type="button">One</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Two</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
          <div class="split-button medium">
            <button class="button tonal">Medium</button>
            <button class="button tonal menu-trigger" data-target="split-medium" aria-label="More options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-medium">
              <li><button type="button">One</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Two</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The seam widens with the size: 4dp through <code>medium</code>, 8dp
          at <code>large</code> and 12dp at <code>xlarge</code>. Use the two
          largest sizes for a split button that is the only thing on a screen.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="split-button large">
            <button class="button tonal">Large</button>
            <button class="button tonal menu-trigger" data-target="split-large" aria-label="More options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-large">
              <li><button type="button">One</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Two</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="split-button xlarge">
            <button class="button tonal">Extra large</button>
            <button class="button tonal menu-trigger" data-target="split-xlarge" aria-label="More options">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
            </button>
            <menu id="split-xlarge">
              <li><button type="button">One</button></li>
              <li><button type="button">Two</button></li>
            </menu>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="split-button medium">
  <button class="button tonal">Medium</button>
  <button class="button tonal menu-trigger" data-target="size-menu" aria-label="More options">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_drop_down</span>
  </button>
  <menu id="size-menu">
    <li><button type="button">One</button></li>
    <li><button type="button">Two</button></li>
  </menu>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          The trailing half is narrower than the leading one, and its chevron
          is larger than a button icon of the same size: 22dp against 20dp at
          the smallest size and 50dp against 40dp at the largest. That keeps
          the trailing half 48dp wide at the two smallest sizes, a full touch
          target, and square at the three largest.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.semantics}>
        <p>
          The container declares no role. A
          <a href="https://github.com/BaezFJ/ExpressiveCSS/blob/master/SEMANTICS.md">composite role</a>
          such as <code>toolbar</code> promises arrow-key navigation, and this
          component leaves it out <em>on purpose</em>, because the two halves
          are independent commands reached with Tab. The only composite widget
          here is the <code>&lt;menu&gt;</code>, which has its own role and
          keyboard model.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trailing half's chevron is decoration, so it is
          <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>, and the button has its own
          <code>aria-label</code>, such as "More save options" rather than
          "More". Name it for what the menu contains, since a screen reader
          announces it right after the leading half's label.
        </p>
        <p>
          Menu sets <code>aria-expanded</code>, so do not author it. The
          constructor adds it to the trigger, every open and close rewrites
          it, and the expanded shape above depends on it. An authored
          <code>"true"</code> draws an open split button over a closed menu.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write both halves as direct child controls: a
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, or an
          <code>&lt;a class="button" href&gt;</code> when it navigates. Do not
          wrap them, because the styles for the seam, the insets and the larger
          chevron target direct children. Do not use an
          <a href={route('icon_buttons')}><code>icon-button</code></a>
          either. It is a separate component that sets its own height, insets,
          colours and icon sizing on the element, so none of this geometry
          applies to it and it conflicts with the seam. The trailing half is a
          <code>&lt;button class="button menu-trigger"&gt;</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Source order matters, and the stylesheet does not check it.</strong>
          The stylesheet tells the halves apart by role rather than by
          position, which is what lets you put the <code>&lt;menu&gt;</code>
          inside the container. As a result, it does not notice a trigger
          written first. That trigger would get the trailing side's round outer
          corners while flex places it on the leading side, so the seam and the
          round ends swap. Put the lead action first, the trigger second, and
          the menu last if it is inside the container. Use exactly two
          controls. A third control also gets the leading half's rules, and a
          container holding only the trigger leaves it squared off along a seam
          with nothing to join. For one control that opens a menu, use a
          <a href={route('menu')}>menu trigger</a> without the
          container. Several peer actions belong in a
          <a href={route('button_groups')}>button group</a>, and
          several related ones belong in the menu.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>
          Set these on the container, or in a rule of your own that picks out
          the split buttons you want to change. The values below are the
          defaults for a small split button. The size classes override all but
          the first two.
        </p>
        <TokenTable>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-between-space</code></td><td><code>2px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-container-shape</code></td><td><code>9999px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-leading-button-trailing-space</code></td><td><code>12px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-trailing-button-space</code></td><td><code>13px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-trailing-icon-size</code></td><td><code>22px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-inner-corner-corner-size</code></td><td><code>4px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-split-button-inner-pressed-corner-corner-size</code></td><td><code>12px</code></td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
        <p>
          Height, the leading half's outer inset, the outline width and every
          colour come from the <a href={route('buttons')}>button</a>
          tokens, which the size class sets on the container for both halves.
          Material specifies separate leading and trailing insets for the
          trailing half. They are equal in all five sizes, so one token covers
          both. The hovered and pressed seam also share one token, because
          Material gives them the same value.
        </p>
  </Section></PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
